---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import GameCard from '../../components/GameCard.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
import games from '../../data/games.json';
import soon from '../../data/coming-soon.json';
import { url } from '../../config';

const logic = games.filter(g => g.category === 'logic-games');
const jsonLd = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: new URL(url(), Astro.site).href },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Memory games', item: new URL(url('logic-games/'), Astro.site).href },
  ],
}];
---
<Base
  title="Free Memory and Logic Games for Kids, Pre-K to Grade 2 | EduLearnLabs"
  description="Free memory and logic games for kids ages 4 to 8: matching pairs, visual memory, patterns and thinking skills. Short, kind, ad-free practice that adapts to your child. No login."
  path="logic-games/" nav="logic" jsonLd={jsonLd}>

  <Breadcrumbs back={{ name: 'Home', path: '' }} items={[{ name: 'Home', path: '' }, { name: 'Memory games', path: 'logic-games/' }]} />

  <h1>Free memory and logic games for kids</h1>
  <p class="lead prose" style="font-size:20px">Short memory and thinking games for Pre-K to Grade 2. Each one practices a
    single skill, like remembering where a picture was or spotting what comes next, takes about 2 to 5 minutes and ends
    with a happy win.</p>

  <div class="grid">
    {logic.map(g => <GameCard game={g} />)}
  </div>

  <h2>Coming soon</h2>
  <ul class="soon-list">
    {soon['logic-games'].map(g => <li>{g.title} <span class="muted">({g.grade})</span></li>)}
  </ul>

  <AdSlot id="logic-mid" />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Why memory and logic games matter</h2>
    <p>Before children read or add, they learn to look carefully, hold things in mind and notice what goes together.
      These quiet thinking skills sit underneath almost everything at school: following a two step direction, finding a
      letter on a page, remembering a number while adding, or seeing that a pattern repeats. Memory and logic games
      practice those skills directly, in a way that feels like play.</p>

    <h2>What is visual memory?</h2>
    <p>Visual memory is the ability to remember what you saw. When a child lifts a flap, sees a ladybug, closes it and
      later remembers "the ladybug was in the top corner", they are using it. Matching pairs games, where cards are
      turned face down and you find two that are the same, are one of the oldest and best ways to build it. Each turn
      asks a child to look, remember a picture and its place, and compare it with a new one.</p>
    <p>Our matching game starts with just two pairs and grows to eight, so even a four year old finishes the first
      board with a smile. Children cannot lose: a flap that does not match closes again and they try again, and the game
      never shows a score of mistakes.</p>

    <h2>What kids learn, grade by grade</h2>
    <h3>Pre-K (ages 4 to 5)</h3>
    <p>Noticing that two pictures are the same, and remembering where a picture is for a few seconds. Small boards of
      two to four pairs are plenty. The goal at this age is to enjoy looking and trying, not to win fast.</p>
    <h3>Kindergarten (ages 5 to 6)</h3>
    <p>Holding more pictures in mind at once, sorting pictures that look alike, and using a simple plan such as "open
      one I have not seen yet". Boards of six to eight pairs fit here. Our
      <a href={url('logic-games/memory-match-for-preschool/')}>memory games for preschool and Kindergarten</a> follow this
      path from a tiny board to a full one.</p>
    <h3>Grade 1 (ages 6 to 7)</h3>
    <p>Finding the odd one out, comparing groups of things and noticing which item does not belong. These games are
      planned next, and they build on the same habit of looking closely.</p>
    <h3>Grade 2 (ages 7 to 8)</h3>
    <p>Continuing a pattern, finding the rule behind it and predicting what comes next. Pattern work is an early
      form of the reasoning used in math and in reading comprehension.</p>

    <h2>Learning goals</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visual memory.</strong> Remember what was seen and where it was.</li>
      <li><strong>Matching.</strong> Decide whether two pictures are the same or only look alike.</li>
      <li><strong>Attention.</strong> Look carefully before choosing, instead of guessing at once.</li>
      <li><strong>Planning.</strong> Use what you already know to choose the next move.</li>
      <li><strong>Persistence.</strong> Miss, try again and finish, without feeling bad about a miss.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How our memory games work</h2>
    <p><strong>One skill, one game.</strong> A matching game only asks your child to find pairs. Clear practice helps a
      child feel progress and helps you see what they can do.</p>
    <p><strong>Hints, never the answer.</strong> If your child keeps missing, Hazel the owl lets them peek at the board
      for a moment, and later makes one flap twinkle. She never matches a pair for them, and nobody sees a failure screen.</p>
    <p><strong>Just right difficulty.</strong> A well remembered board and the game moves to a bigger one. A board that
      needed a lot of help and it steps back. A new picture layout is made each time, so your child practices
      remembering, not memorizing one picture grid.</p>
    <p><strong>A reward that stays calm.</strong> Every finished board adds a patch to your child's own quilt, kept on
      this device. There are no timers, no streaks and no scores to beat.</p>

    <h2>How parents can help</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Play the first board together and talk about what you see: "the snail was on the left".</li>
      <li>Take turns for a few boards, then let your child lead. Say your plan out loud: "I will open a new one."</li>
      <li>Do not rush. A child who looks for a while is practicing the skill.</li>
      <li>Short and often beats long and rare. One game a day is plenty.</li>
      <li>Play pretend memory games away from the screen, like "what is missing?" with three toys on a tray.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Want to try other skills? Spell short words in
      <a href={url('language-games/word-building-for-grade-1/')}>Word Building for Grade 1</a>, or practice numbers with
      <a href={url('math-games/addition-for-grade-1/')}>Addition for Grade 1</a> and
      <a href={url('math-games/subtraction-for-grade-1/')}>Subtraction for Grade 1</a>.</p>

    <h2>Questions parents ask</h2>
    <h3>Are the memory games really free?</h3>
    <p>Yes. Every game is free to play, with no ads inside games, no in-game purchases and nothing to unlock with money.</p>
    <h3>My child cannot read yet. Can they still play?</h3>
    <p>Yes. The matching game uses pictures and a short spoken line, with no reading at all.</p>
    <h3>Does my child need an account?</h3>
    <p>No. There is no login and no sign up. Progress and the quilt are saved only in this browser on this device, and
      you can clear them any time on the <a href={url('grown-ups/')}>Grown-ups page</a>.</p>
    <h3>Which devices work?</h3>
    <p>Any modern browser: tablets, phones held sideways, laptops and Chromebooks. The games are small and load in a few
      seconds, even on older school computers and slow connections.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
